 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360340 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c88eff33-055e-5cf0-9bab-aa2a49a23d0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360370 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7f6bb20-e87d-5e8c-86a3-f4afdfd0d828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360401 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e3a73fb3-c55f-5cfb-9539-d9135bd7d9a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $F.$\n- For the zero polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F$ such that $p(x)+q(x)\\ne q(x)+p(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "998609c4-bfb7-5e7d-ad89-b03b40316ca2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360471 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot x+0.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in F,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in F,$ we have that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of polynomials is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $U$ be the set of functions of the form $p(x)=ax,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).$\n- Multiplication by $1$ does not change elements in $U.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa6bb928-4008-5011-93e0-eb9181dde1e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360481 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.$\n- The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n- For any $A\\in P,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9c8bcb7-0a52-5b49-83ac-484cd54a7853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369915 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}-\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\notin P\n$$\n\nbecause $-1+0≯0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369916 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7526c0f6-b5f9-5239-86c3-4d3e5b6a45bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369917 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64ef1014-64aa-5fbd-816c-4bb255259696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369918 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98e1bde9-bd4e-5068-945b-089233c0ce59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369919 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be43e8a4-3648-5ea0-89ef-321148195a63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369920 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4373c573-5a80-56fc-8a9c-15f194e90e8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369921 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "572bd323-09ef-5173-aed5-096769e1278c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $E$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a\\,\\text{ }{2}^{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c4d0420-8d1b-51e3-ab5c-ec13f5c75afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371264 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371265 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371265 #edb/ref 10